163
00:06:13,919 --> 00:06:18,560
We introduce two families of methods,

164
00:06:15,919 --> 00:06:20,319
algebraic and analytic. Let's begin with

165
00:06:18,560 --> 00:06:24,520
the algebraic approach and set up the

166
00:06:20,319 --> 00:06:24,520
measurement model it relies on.

167
00:06:25,759 --> 00:06:30,400
The measurement can be written in line

168
00:06:27,520 --> 00:06:32,080
integral form or in ray sum form. Let me

169
00:06:30,400 --> 00:06:34,319
explain.

170
00:06:32,080 --> 00:06:36,960
First, we have an incoming intensity ni

171
00:06:34,319 --> 00:06:38,800
and an output intensity n o. The output

172
00:06:36,960 --> 00:06:41,280
is related to the input through linear

173
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:44,160
attenuation. For a single element along

174
00:06:41,280 --> 00:06:48,560
the ray, the attenuation is mu* delta x

175
00:06:44,160 --> 00:06:50,639
where delta x is the pixel size.

176
00:06:48,560 --> 00:06:52,720
With multiple material components along

177
00:06:50,639 --> 00:06:54,880
the path, the incoming intensity is

178
00:06:52,720 --> 00:06:56,720
attenuated by the first pixel. The

179
00:06:54,880 --> 00:06:59,039
output of the first pixel becomes the

180
00:06:56,720 --> 00:07:00,880
input to the second pixel and so on.

181
00:06:59,039 --> 00:07:03,199
Adding these effects together gives the

182
00:07:00,880 --> 00:07:07,039
standard relationship. This is a quick

183
00:07:03,199 --> 00:07:09,520
review of the earlier module.

184
00:07:07,039 --> 00:07:11,680
The attenuated output equals the input

185
00:07:09,520 --> 00:07:13,919
multiplied by an exponential factor

186
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:16,639
whose exponent is the negative sum of

187
00:07:13,919 --> 00:07:19,360
the little muk values each weighted by

188
00:07:16,639 --> 00:07:21,599
delta x. This is our imaging model or

189
00:07:19,360 --> 00:07:23,440
data model. What is known and what is

190
00:07:21,599 --> 00:07:26,160
unknown?

191
00:07:23,440 --> 00:07:29,039
Known n i n o and the step size delta x

192
00:07:26,160 --> 00:07:32,880
that we choose. Unknown. The set mu k

193
00:07:29,039 --> 00:07:34,960
for k= 1 2 3 e n

194
00:07:32,880 --> 00:07:38,639
normalize by taking a ratio and then a

195
00:07:34,960 --> 00:07:42,880
natural logarithm. Natural log of ni / n

196
00:07:38,639 --> 00:07:44,479
equals the sum over k of mu k * delta x.

197
00:07:42,880 --> 00:07:47,039
This is a linear equation in the

198
00:07:44,479 --> 00:07:49,599
unknowns mu k with delta x acting as a

199
00:07:47,039 --> 00:07:51,520
weight. Because ni and n o are measured,

200
00:07:49,599 --> 00:07:54,479
the right hand side is known after the

201
00:07:51,520 --> 00:07:56,960
log. For each x-ray path, we obtain one

202
00:07:54,479 --> 00:07:58,720
linear equation. With many paths, we

203
00:07:56,960 --> 00:08:02,400
obtain a system of linear equations

204
00:07:58,720 --> 00:08:04,319
often written compactly as a * x= b. In

205
00:08:02,400 --> 00:08:06,319
this algebraic view, the image is

206
00:08:04,319 --> 00:08:08,319
treated as pixels and each measurement

207
00:08:06,319 --> 00:08:10,560
