projection, you smear back the other

1364
00:53:47,040 --> 00:53:51,520
way. You add the two together. Many

1365
00:53:49,760 --> 00:53:54,520
projections add many contributions

1366
00:53:51,520 --> 00:53:54,520
together.

1367
00:53:56,079 --> 00:54:00,800
from 180 degrees. You add everything

1368
00:53:58,319 --> 00:54:02,480
together, you get something not too much

1369
00:54:00,800 --> 00:54:04,400
different from the truth, but it looks

1370
00:54:02,480 --> 00:54:06,240
very blurred,

1371
00:54:04,400 --> 00:54:07,920
you still see a region that is a little

1372
00:54:06,240 --> 00:54:10,319
high because this part of the projection

1373
00:54:07,920 --> 00:54:12,480
is high. When you back project along the

1374
00:54:10,319 --> 00:54:15,040
original X-ray paths, that high value is

1375
00:54:12,480 --> 00:54:17,119
placed along those lines. However, there

1376
00:54:15,040 --> 00:54:19,280
are artifacts

1377
00:54:17,119 --> 00:54:21,119
in the smearing back process. A high

1378
00:54:19,280 --> 00:54:23,440
value here gets spread to places that

1379
00:54:21,119 --> 00:54:25,359
are not actually high in the object.

1380
00:54:23,440 --> 00:54:27,760
Back projection alone smears the high

1381
00:54:25,359 --> 00:54:29,680
values around. So simply collecting

1382
00:54:27,760 --> 00:54:32,240
projections and doing back projection is

1383
00:54:29,680 --> 00:54:34,000
not enough to recover an accurate image.

1384
00:54:32,240 --> 00:54:37,000
That is why we really need to filter the

1385
00:54:34,000 --> 00:54:37,000
projections.

1386
00:54:38,720 --> 00:54:43,359
So you have a projection profile. You

1387
00:54:41,040 --> 00:54:45,839
don't just smear it back. First apply a

1388
00:54:43,359 --> 00:54:47,680
highpass filter with kernel H. After

1389
00:54:45,839 --> 00:54:50,000
filtering you get a highpass filtered

1390
00:54:47,680 --> 00:54:51,920
projection profile called P prime of

1391
00:54:50,000 --> 00:54:54,400
theta T.

1392
00:54:51,920 --> 00:54:56,640
Then you perform back projection. That's

1393
00:54:54,400 --> 00:54:59,280
filtered back projection. Filter each

1394
00:54:56,640 --> 00:55:03,319
projection with H and back project every

1395
00:54:59,280 --> 00:55:03,319
filtered projection profile.

1396
00:55:05,040 --> 00:55:09,440
This is the synog. The filtering is

1397
00:55:07,440 --> 00:55:11,520
one-dimensional along the horizontal

1398
00:55:09,440 --> 00:55:14,160
direction. So a highpass filter

1399
00:55:11,520 --> 00:55:16,000
emphasizes edges.

1400
00:55:14,160 --> 00:55:18,240
Here is the original synogram. Here's

1401
00:55:16,000 --> 00:55:20,160
the filtered centagram. Once it is

1402
00:55:18,240 --> 00:55:23,040
filtered, we use these data for back

1403
00:55:20,160 --> 00:55:23,040
projection.

1404
00:55:24,720 --> 00:55:29,280
then you can recover the original image.

1405
00:55:27,200 --> 00:55:31,440
This looks very close to the original.

1406
00:55:29,280 --> 00:55:33,359
If you have many angles with very small

1407
00:55:31,440 --> 00:55:35,440
angular increments and the detector

1408
00:55:33,359 --> 00:55:37,599
spacing is very small, the result

1409
00:55:35,440 --> 00:55:39,359
becomes closer and closer to the true

1410
00:55:37,599 --> 00:55:41,760
image.

1411
00:55:39,359 --> 00:55:43,760
This is just a numerical example. The

1412
00:55:41,760 --> 00:55:46,240
next few slides show a mat lab

1413
00:55:43,760 --> 00:55:47,680
implementation, also your homework, so

1414
00:55:46,240 --> 00:55:50,680
you can read through and try it

1415
00:55:47,680 --> 00:55:50,680
yourself.

1416
00:55:52,240 --> 00:55:55,760
Mat Lab has toolbox commands that

1417
00:55:54,160 --> 00:55:58,240
implement projection, filtering and

1418
00:55:55,760 --> 00:56:00,720
filtered back projection.

1419
00:55:58,240 --> 00:56:02,960
The keyword is rayon. You supply an

1420
00:56:00,720 --> 00:56:04,960
image in theta. Theta can be a single

1421
00:56:02,960 --> 00:56:09,160
value which gives one projection or an

1422
00:56:04,960 --> 00:56:09,160
array which gives many projections.

1423
00:56:10,880 --> 00:56:15,200
The inverse transformation is done with

1424
00:56:12,799 --> 00:56:17,440
Iridon. The projection beams are laid

1425
00:56:15,200 --> 00:56:22,440
out line by line and the default line

1426
00:56:17,440 --> 00:56:22,440
spacing is one pixel. one unit apart.

1427
00:56:24,000 --> 00:56:28,720
The setup looks like this. Parallel beam

1428
00:56:26,480 --> 00:56:30,319
geometry with sensors on one side, a

1429
00:56:28,720 --> 00:56:32,559
source on the other, and the system

1430
00:56:30,319 --> 00:56:35,559
rotating by the angle theta to collect

1431
00:56:32,559 --> 00:56:35,559
projections.

1432
00:56:37,280 --> 00:56:41,920
When you do projection, they basically

1433
00:56:39,280 --> 00:56:44,000
decompose a single pixel into a 2x2

1434
00:56:41,920 --> 00:56:46,240
matrix, then shoot rays through all

1435
00:56:44,000 --> 00:56:48,079
these centers into the projection

1436
00:56:46,240 --> 00:56:50,000
domain.

1437
00:56:48,079 --> 00:56:52,480
If a ray hits the center of a detector

1438
00:56:50,000 --> 00:56:54,240
bin, that detector takes the full value.

1439
00:56:52,480 --> 00:56:56,079
If it hits the boundary between two

1440
00:56:54,240 --> 00:56:58,559
detector bins, the value is divided

1441
00:56:56,079 --> 00:57:03,720
equally. If it hits at an arbitrary

1442
00:56:58,559 --> 00:57:03,720
location, linear interpolation is used.

1443
00:57:05,200 --> 00:57:09,599
Here's a sample radon code you can run

1444
00:57:07,280 --> 00:57:11,119
yourself. It generates an image with a

1445
00:57:09,599 --> 00:57:13,440
white block, then produces the

1446
00:57:11,119 --> 00:57:15,680
projection data.

1447
00:57:13,440 --> 00:57:17,760
After that, you apply the filtration.

1448
00:57:15,680 --> 00:57:21,880
The red arrows simply point to figures

1449
00:57:17,760 --> 00:57:21,880
that appear on the next slide.

1450
00:57:23,359 --> 00:57:28,119
You get this projection profile, the

1451
00:57:25,119 --> 00:57:28,119
synog.

1452
00:57:29,760 --> 00:57:33,680
Next, use the inverse radon code which

1453
00:57:32,079 --> 00:57:36,000
essentially performs filtered back

1454
00:57:33,680 --> 00:57:37,680
projection. You can also choose linear

1455
00:57:36,000 --> 00:57:39,760
interpolation. And if you set the filter

1456
00:57:37,680 --> 00:57:43,400
to none, that means no filtering, just

1457
00:57:39,760 --> 00:57:43,400
simple back projection.

1458
00:57:45,040 --> 00:57:48,559
On the top row images, you can see the

1459
00:57:46,960 --> 00:57:50,640
difference. The unfiltered back

1460
00:57:48,559 --> 00:57:52,799
projection is blurry, while the filtered

1461
00:57:50,640 --> 00:57:54,960
back projection looks much closer to the

1462
00:57:52,799 --> 00:57:57,760
original image. This is the key to

1463
00:57:54,960 --> 00:57:59,359
reconstructing CT images.

